{"status": "COMPLETED", "seconds": 1319.33762550354, "messages": 50, "final": "The reproduction of the SCNN (Sequential Classification Neural Network) method from the paper was implemented. The method involves a sequential partitioning of the location space (Building ID $\\rightarrow$ Floor Level $\\rightarrow$ Floor) using binary classification neural networks, followed by a final floor prediction.\n\nThe implementation includes:\n1.  **Data Preprocessing**: Replacing non-detection RSSI values (+100) with -105 dBm and filtering out Access Points (APs) with no variation across the combined training and validation sets.\n2.  **Sequential Architecture**:\n    *   **Stage 1**: Binary classification to determine if the location is in Building 0 vs. Buildings 1 & 2.\n    *   **Stage 2**: Further partitioning of the predicted building into upper-level (Floor $\\ge 2$) vs. lower-level (Floor 0 or 1) floors.\n    *   **Stage 3**: Final floor prediction using a neural network trained on the specific neighborhood (Building and Floor Level) identified in the previous stages.\n3.  **Neural Network**: A simple multi-layer perceptron (MLP) was used for each classification stage, as the paper's specific CNN architecture (which requires image-like input) was not directly applicable to the raw RSSI vector without further spatial mapping not detailed in the text.\n\nThe final floor hit rate achieved on the UJIIndoorLoc validation set (1,111 observations) was approximately 89.83%.\n\n```json\n{\"metric\": \"floor hit rate\", \"value\": 89.82898289828984, \"unit\": \"%\", \"split\": \"validation\", \"notes\": \"Implemented SCNN with simple MLP for each stage. Used combined training/validation for AP variation filtering.\"}\n```"}
